+# Billing service
+
+`BillingService` (`src/service.py`) is the service layer for billing reads and
+writes. Data is tenant-scoped; `src/repo.py` holds an in-memory stand-in for
+the billing database, and `src/cache.py` holds the in-process cache.
+
+Run the tests with `python -m pytest`.
+
+## Caching
+
+`BillingService` caches some reads in an in-process `Cache`. The cache lives
+in the memory of a single process: each `BillingService` gets its own `Cache`
+unless one is passed in, and nothing is shared between processes or servers.
+
+### Which reads are cached
+
+| Read                  | Cached? | Cached per            |
+|-----------------------|---------|-----------------------|
+| `get_customer`        | Yes     | tenant + customer     |
+| `get_invoice_summary` | Yes     | tenant + period       |
+| `get_total_paid`      | Yes     | tenant + customer     |
+| `get_balance`         | No      | always read from DB   |
+| `list_open_invoices`  | No      | always read from DB   |
+| Any read for tenant `internal` | No | always read from DB |
+
+Why some reads always go to the database:
+
+- **Tenant `internal`**: its data is regenerated on every request by the audit
+  service, and showing stale values is a compliance issue (SOX control C-14).
+  Nothing for this tenant is ever cached.
+- **`get_balance` and `list_open_invoices`**: these skip the cache because of a
+  historical workaround for cross-tenant data leaks (BILL-2291). Every call to
+  either of them also **clears the whole cache, for all tenants**. The
+  underlying leak has since been fixed (see "Tenant isolation" below), so this
+  is no longer needed for isolation, but it is still in place. In practice it
+  means balances and open-invoice lists are always current.
+
+### What invalidates cached data, and when
+
+- **Writes through the service**: `upsert_customer`, `create_invoice` and
+  `mark_invoice_paid` clear the entire cache (every tenant, not just the one
+  written to) as soon as the database write succeeds. The next read of
+  anything reloads from the database.
+- **Calls to `get_balance` / `list_open_invoices`**: clear the entire cache, as
+  described above.
+- **Time-to-live (TTL)**: each cached entry expires once it is `ttl_seconds`
+  old (default **300 seconds / 5 minutes**), counted from when the value was
+  loaded from the database. The next read after expiry reloads it and starts a
+  new TTL. `ttl_seconds=0` turns caching off; negative values are rejected.
+
+### How stale can data be?
+
+- **Changes made through the same service instance**: visible on the very next
+  read — no staleness.
+- **Changes that bypass the service**: for example back-office tools editing
+  the database directly (BILL-2324), or writes handled by a different process
+  or server (which has its own cache). A cached `get_customer`,
+  `get_invoice_summary` or `get_total_paid` result can lag behind these for up
+  to `ttl_seconds` — **5 minutes with the default settings**.
+- **Concurrent requests**: the service does no locking, so if a read is loading
+  from the database at the same moment a write happens, the pre-write value can
+  end up cached. This is also bounded by the TTL.
+- `get_balance`, `list_open_invoices` and anything for tenant `internal` are
+  never stale, because they are never cached.
+
+For support: if a customer reports a wrong customer record, invoice summary or
+total-paid figure after a change made outside the normal service path, it
+should correct itself within 5 minutes. Balances and open-invoice lists should
+always be current.
+
+### Tenant isolation
+
+A single cache holds data for all tenants. Data is kept apart by the cache key:
+every key is `(tenant_id, operation, entity_id)` (see `make_key` in
+`src/cache.py`). This matters because customer ids and billing periods are only
+unique within a tenant — two tenants can both have customer `c-1` or period
+`2024-05`, and the tenant id in the key ensures each tenant only ever sees its
+own cached values. Cache clears on writes affect all tenants, but that only
+causes extra database reads; it never mixes data between tenants.
